course: everyday
lesson: 470
title: The friend who only calls when they need something
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Connor's friend Ollie only messages when he needs something: help with maths homework, a lend of a charger, Connor's spare ticket to the match. When Connor invites him to his birthday, Ollie is «busy, soz». Connor's mum finds him moping. «Friends who only call for favours are like a tap that only runs one way, love. You end up empty.» Connor stops jumping every time Ollie pings. Ollie stops messaging. It goes quiet, and Connor realises he doesn't miss him much. He spends more time with Raj, who never asks for anything and always brings two biscuits.
explain: Kieran's mate Deano «forgets» his dinner money every week. «Bare skint again, bro, I'll sort you Friday.» «Bare» is London slang for very, «skint» means out of money. Friday never comes; he owes £7.40. Kieran starts saying, «Sorry, I need it today.» Deano grumbles that Kieran's «tight» — stingy — and then stops asking. Jake's cousin Callum rings only to borrow Jake's PlayStation «for my mate's sleepover, cuz». Last time it came back on Thursday, not Monday, with a sticky controller. This time Jake says, «Nah, but come round and play here.» Callum comes, and it's actually a laugh.
explain: Some favours are wrong to do at all. Maddie's friend Jess phones only when she needs cover: «Just tell my mum I'm at yours, yeah? Love you, babe.» Jess is going round the shops with older girls. Maddie says no. Jess calls her «boring» and «a snake», and stops calling. Maddie feels a bit lonely — and right; her mum hugs her and says she's proud. In tests Bella whispers «what did you get for number 6?» and Chloe tells her, but when Chloe asks for help with fractions, Bella is «too busy». Chloe stops whispering answers, and finds a study buddy, Aisha, who helps with fractions and asks for help with spelling. Lily's friend Hannah only invites her round the night before a test, to revise Hannah's weak bits — never Saturday, never fun. Lily says, «I'll help, but let's do something fun after.» They bake terrible cookies, and it's the best afternoon they've had.
explain: Mia's friend Ava turns up whenever Mia has a new game, «just to try it». When Mia's grandpa is in hospital, Ava doesn't text once. «A fair-weather friend is here for the sunshine and gone when it rains, sugar,» Grandma says. Mia notices who did text: Kenzie, twice a day. Sam lends Rafi his best football boots for the cup match; they come back caked in mud, a lace snapped, a split in the toe. «My bad, bro.» No offer to fix them. Next day Rafi brings new laces — but the split stays. Kieran's neighbour Shannon is lovely to him exactly when the ice cream van plays: «Kieran, you're my favourite, get us a 99?» The rest of the week she ignores him. He stops buying. She stops being nice. She was never really nice at all.
fact: Friendship goes both ways: both people give and both people take.
fact: Someone who only gets in touch when they want something is a one-way friend.
fact: You can say no to lending money or things; a real friend pays back and looks after what they borrow.
fact: Lend only what you would be all right about losing, and ask for it back looked after.
fact: Never lie to a parent to cover for a friend; a real friend shouldn't ask you to.
fact: Giving someone your test answers is cheating and can get you both into trouble; test answers stay your own.
fact: Asking for balance, like «I'll help, but let's do something fun after», can fix a one-way friendship.
fact: Turning a favour into time together shows whether a person wanted you or only your things.
fact: A fair-weather friend is around for the good times and disappears when things get hard.
fact: Real friends show up on bad days too; notice who does.
fact: If someone's kindness has a price tag, it isn't really kindness.
fact: Saying no to a one-way friend can feel lonely at first, but it leaves room for friends who give back.
fact: «Bare» is London slang for very, «skint» means out of money, «tight» means stingy, and «my bad» means «my mistake».
exercise: When did Ollie message Connor? | Only when he needed something | Every day, just to chat and see how he was | Only on Connor's birthday | Never at all
exercise: Ollie was «busy, soz» for Connor's birthday. What does that show? | It was a one-way friendship | Ollie was very kind | Ollie loved birthdays | Connor forgot to invite him
exercise: What did Connor's mum mean by «a tap that only runs one way»? | One person gives and the other only takes | The kitchen tap was broken and needed a plumber | Ollie wastes water | Friends should drink more water
exercise: What happened when Connor stopped jumping for Ollie? | Ollie stopped messaging, and Connor didn't miss him much | Ollie started asking how Connor was and became his best friend | Ollie paid him back | Connor got lonely forever
exercise: «Bare skint again, bro.» What is Deano saying? | He's got no money at all again | He's very cold and shivering again | He's lost his shoes again | He's very happy again
exercise: How did Kieran stop lending to Deano? | Said «Sorry, I need it today» | Hid his dinner money in his sock | Told the head teacher about him | Borrowed money from Deano instead
exercise: What did Jake offer Callum instead of lending the PlayStation? | To come round and play at Jake's | To sell it to him | To lend it for a whole month instead of a night | Nothing, he hung up
exercise: Jess asked Maddie to tell her mum she was at Maddie's. What did Maddie do? | Said no | Lied for her | Went to the shops too | Told Jess's mum a different lie
exercise: Why was Maddie right to say no to Jess? | A friend shouldn't ask you to lie for them | Shopping is against the law | Maddie's mum was out | Jess wasn't really her friend anyway, so it didn't matter
exercise: Bella asks for test answers but won't help Chloe with fractions. What did Chloe do? | Stopped whispering answers and found a fair study buddy | Gave her even more answers, hoping she'd help back one day | Copied Bella's answers back | Stopped going to school
exercise: Why should test answers stay your own? | Sharing them is cheating and gets you both in trouble | Answers are worth money | Teachers can hear whispers from the other side of the room | Bella's answers were wrong
exercise: What is a fair-weather friend? | Someone there for good times, gone for bad | Someone who loves sunny days and hates rain | A friend who lives far away | A friend who checks the weather
exercise: Who texted Mia when her grandpa was in hospital? | Kenzie, twice a day | Ava, every single hour | Nobody at all, all week | Her teacher from school
exercise: Rafi returned Sam's boots ruined. What is the lesson about lending? | Lend only what you'd be OK losing, and ask for care | Never lend anything to anyone, not even your best mate | Always lend your best things | Make people pay to borrow
exercise: Why was Shannon only nice when the ice cream van came? | She wanted Kieran to buy her ice cream | She loves the van's tune and sings along to it | She liked Kieran a lot | She was hot in the sun
exercise: What does Shannon's story teach? | Kindness with a price tag isn't kindness | Ice cream makes people kind, at least for a bit | Always buy friends a 99 | Neighbours are always fake
exercise: Lily's friend Hannah only invites her to revise Hannah's weak bits. What fixed it? | «I'll help, but let's do something fun after» | Doing Hannah's homework for her so they could play instead | Refusing to ever revise again | Telling Hannah she's thick
test: «u never lend me stuff, ur well tight». Is it wrong to say no to lending? | No, you can say no, and real friends pay back | Yes, friends must lend everything | Yes, saying no is rude | No, but you must give it away instead
test: Jess: «just cover for me, babe, it's one little lie». What would most likely happen if Maddie did it every week? | Both get into big trouble when it comes out | Nothing, little lies like that never come out | Jess's mum would thank her | Maddie would become popular
test: Mia's sad and Ava hasn't texted once, but she was round every day for the new game. What does that tell Mia? | Ava is a fair-weather friend | Ava's phone is broken | Ava is shy about sadness | Ava is her truest friend
test: «he only rings when he wants summat, but he's still my mate». How could Connor test that? | Invite him to something with nothing to gain | Lend him even more stuff and see if he says thanks | Buy him a present | Never speak to him again
test: In a cartoon, the hero keeps doing favours for a «friend» who never helps back, and the friend suddenly changes. What's more real? | One-way friends usually stay one-way unless you ask for balance | If you keep doing favours long enough, they always change by themselves | Doing more favours fixes it | Cartoons are always true
test: Rafi: «my bad, bro» about the split boot. What would a fair friend do? | Offer to fix or help replace it | Say «my bad» and walk off | Borrow the other boot as well | Blame the mud on the pitch for it
test: Bella: «what did u get for 6? quick». What's the best reply? | «Sorry, can't, do your own» | Whisper the answer quickly | Whisper a wrong answer to trick her | Shout it across the class
test: Kieran stopped buying Shannon 99s and she stopped being nice. What does that show? | Her kindness was only about the ice cream | Kieran was mean to stop and should say sorry | Shannon got ill | Shannon loves Kieran secretly
test: What would most likely have happened if Kieran kept lending Deano money every week? | Kieran would keep losing money | Deano would pay it all back Friday | Deano would start lending to Kieran | The debt would vanish by itself
test: Callum wants the PlayStation for a sleepover. Jake says «come play here». Why is that a clever answer? | It shows if Callum wanted Jake or just the stuff | It means Jake gets a new PlayStation | It gets Callum into trouble | It's the same as lending it
test: «Raj never asks for anything and always brings two biscuits». What kind of friend is Raj? | A friend who gives as well as takes | A friend who wants something | A fair-weather friend | A frenemy
exam: Identify the principal characteristic of a friendship described as running «one way». | One party habitually receives favours whilst giving nothing in return | Both parties exchange favours in equal measure | Neither party requests any assistance | The parties communicate solely by telephone
exam: Given that a friend requests that a pupil deceive the friend's parent as to her whereabouts, ascertain the most appropriate response. | To decline to deceive the parent | To comply with the request on this occasion only | To accompany the friend in order to supervise her | To inform the parent of a different falsehood
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a «fair-weather friend»? | One who is present in favourable times and absent in adversity | One whose company is enjoyed chiefly in summer | One who resides in a warmer country | One who supports a friend chiefly in adversity
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the lending of possessions to friends. | One should lend only what one could bear to lose, and request its careful return | One is obliged to lend any item requested by a friend, whatever its value to oneself | Lending invariably strengthens a friendship | Items lent need never be returned in good condition
